The category filter works reasonably on mobile but looks inconsistent in tablet and desktop layouts.

Some chips shift vertically, text becomes clipped, labels are not fully visible, and spacing is inconsistent. The same filter should look intentional at every viewport size.

### Required behavior

Make the category filter a stable, responsive horizontal row.

- All category chips must remain properly aligned.
- Chip text must remain fully visible.
- Do not allow labels to wrap into two lines unexpectedly.
- Avoid clipped words, clipped letters, partial chip borders, and vertical misalignment.
- Keep chip heights consistent.
- Use appropriate horizontal padding, gaps, line-height, and border radii.
- Apply box-sizing consistently.
- Remove conflicting fixed widths, absolute positioning, or inherited styles that cause the bar to break at particular breakpoints.
- Do not force all chips to squeeze into a narrow row if there is insufficient space.

For narrow phones and tablets, allow the filter row to scroll horizontally when necessary. Keep the active chip visible and prevent the webpage itself from developing unwanted horizontal overflow.

For wider desktop layouts, display the chips in a stable, balanced row with consistent spacing. Centering the row is acceptable when it fits comfortably.

On every breakpoint, ensure the filter remains correctly positioned below the header.

Test the filter around these widths:

320px, 360px, 390px, 430px, 768px, 820px, 1024px, 1280px, and 1440px.

Include intermediate widths where wrapping or layout changes are likely to occur.

